      "text": "Let's define a variable to represent the number of digits in the positive integers with digits that add up to 11. Let x represent the number of digits in these positive integers.\n\nThe smallest positive integer with $x$ digits is 1 followed by $x-1$ zeros: $10^{x-1}$. The next smallest is 2 followed by $x-2$ zeros:  $2 \\cdot 10^{x-2}$. In general, the smallest positive integer with $x$ digits is $x \\cdot 10^{x-1}$.\n\nThe total value of all positive integers with $x$ digits is the sum of $x \\cdot 10^{x-1}$ for each digit place from 1 to 11.\n\nThe sum of the digits is the total of all positive integers with each place held by a digit and the total value of the numbers:\n\n\\[100\\cdots x \\cdot 10^{x-1} + 90\\cdots(x - 1) \\cdot 10^{x-2} + 80\\cdots(x - 2) \\cdot 10^{x-3} + \\cdots + 20\\cdots1 \\cdot 10^0 + 10\\cdots1\\]\n\nSince each digit is from 1 to 9, we can simplify the problem by recognizing that the number will always be greater than or equal to 15, the smallest number formed by 1 digit greater than 1. This allows a recurrence relation from close to 15 to larger numbers.\n\nRepresenting $f(x)$ as the function that gives the value for the number of positive integers whose digits add up to $11$ with $x$ digits, which is $x \\cdot 10^x$, we can consider $f(n+1)$ for any $x$.\n\nBy adding another digit to a number that has $x$ digits and the digits add up to 10, we can create all numbers with x+1 digits and digits adding up to 10 with $f(x+1)$, for a recurrence relation to help in solving this problem.\n\n\nBy adding an extra digit to 10, which equates to adding $10$ to the total digits. A number with just 1 digit is 9 ten copies make 90.\n\nStarting with $f(1)=90 \\\\ f(2)=288 \\\\ f(3)=768 \\\\ f(4)=1761 \\\\ f(5)=0$\n\nThe base case is 90 (9 1's). Therefore placing one more digit to 90 or 100 make a number with 11 digits. There is $10 \\cdot 10^9 = 100 \\cdot 10^9$ digits that 10 increases by, so we amount to:\n\n$f(11) = 90 + 3 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 10^9 = \\boxed{9999999990}$\n\nLet's verify our result by finding the correct placement of an extra digit in the integer:\n\nTake $90 + 1 = 91$, we can place the number as $f(11) = \\boxed{9999999990}$\nThe answer is: 9999999990",
