      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The smallest number possible is 110 and the largest is 922. Since the eleventh number is one tenth of the largest number, it's 92. Thus, the answer is \\boxed{92}.\n\nTo get confused, the way I did it was as follows: the largest number with 11 digits that add up to 11 is 9999999994. Since 999999999 > 922 I concluded it would be 922. The way I originally did it, it seems pretty clear that the first digit (leftmost one) cannot be 9. But after solving it and looking on AoPS, I realized that the biggest one must be 922. But upon checking the answers, it will be 922. After rereading the problem I realized I negated the condition on the digits and not the actual number so the answer is 92. The correct answer is 92\n\nTo check my work, I made a 2-line table in excel. I then found my answer, 92.\nI hope this helps!\n\nNext time, make sure to include where you got the observations from on a spreadsheet.\n\nWell, 92 was given as the answer... With one extra 9... Anyway, if you list all positive integers whose digits add up to k, you're going to find that the numbers grow by 9 every time you go to the next line. The 92 is our tenth line, a half line shorter than the 9498, which corresponds to the eleventh line.\n\nBy the way, you don't need to square root the equation $\\sum_{n = 0}^{11} 11 = n(12 - n)$ to find the average since the middle term is $n = 6, 11$, so the middle term is simply $$3(12) = \\boxed{36}$$",
