      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine how many numbers have digit sums that match our running total. 11 is just under a seven-digit number so we evaluate smaller digits, and we increase our mathematically solving powers as we double.\n\nIf a number in our list is N numbers wide, adding all of the digits gives us a sum of $d$, so $d < 11$. In order to determine the card number, we need to accurately calculate its counter and see if it exceeds eleven digits. 11 digits means the sum of the digits seen is 77. Luckily, there are only $77$ digits in a $7$-digit number. If any number exceeds $7$, we just find its closest buddy.\n\n<image>\n\nThe first number to have a digit sum of 11 is $d = 10 + 1$, which is the integer part of $11$ digits wide:\n\n$a = \\lfloor \\sqrt{11}\\rfloor = 4$\n\nBy summing up 2:4:\n\n$2 + 1 = 3$\n\nThis number having a digit sum of 11 is:\n\n$14_{11} = 711$\n\nNow, we evaluate this number in terms of digits:\n\n$d = 4, s = 3$\n\nIf we combine these to:\n\n$43_{11}$, we get a number greater than 76 and we need to add 7 to since the rest of the digits sum to 1 to make this a $7$ digit. So, $15$ gets a digit sum:\n\n$15_{11} = 78_9$\n\nAll the numbers should be in ascending sequence, so we just have to pick them. This is the 11 count.\n\nFor a total of:\n\n$$\\boxed{711}$$",
